DESIGN in the rationale (DECISION, SUPER CALLS). Steps:
1. graph/src/calls.rs emit_abstract_implements -> emit_override_implements(g, stats): the walk is unchanged (superclasses(g) in key order, the sub's own methods sorted by name, nearest_definer per name); `Definer::One(declared)` pairs (sub_mid, declared, rule) with rule = if is_abstract_method(g, declared) { "abstract_override" } else { "concrete_override" }, skipping a name equal to "constructor" (a constructor is invoked by `new` and `super(..)`, never dispatched). Pairs: Vec<(NodeId, NodeId, &'static str)>, sorted by (from.0, to.0), deduped on (from, to), existing IMPLEMENTS (from, to) dropped, then push_edge_with(g, from, to, edge_category::IMPLEMENTS, graph_evidence("graph:iface", rule), Confidence::Strong) (an explicit `extends` plus the same name, as abstract_override; unlocated like it, so why::tier_of reads DERIVED, "no location recorded"). stats.implements counts abstract pairs, the new stats.concrete counts concrete ones. Doc: the method-level IMPLEMENTS of an override names the member it replaces at dispatch: an abstract declaration (CH.1b) or a concrete body (DF.22c), told apart by the rule. graph/src/build.rs: the `use crate::calls::{..}` list and build_ts_like's call site rename (two lines); no other builder calls it.
2. resolve_inherited_calls: the One(to) arm stamps graph_evidence("graph:calls", if is_super { "super_method" } else { "inherited_method" }); the doc says a `super.m()` is a non-virtual call (it runs exactly the parent's member) and why dead_symbol's dispatch rule tells it apart. DF.22b's inherited_receiver arm is unchanged.
3. InheritStats: `concrete: usize`; marker `[ts-inherit] calls bound through superclasses: <n> (self=<s> super=<p> receiver=<r> ambiguous=<a>) abstract implements=<m> concrete overrides=<c>`, printed when any count > 0.
4. engine/src/gaps.rs View::build: in the existing edge loop collect `calls_into: HashMap<NodeId, Vec<&'a Edge>>` - every CALLS / USES edge with from != to whose `to` is in `methods`, in edge order; the dispatch loop's test becomes `calls_into.get(&e.to).is_some_and(|es| es.iter().any(|c| c.from != e.from && !is_super_call(c)))`. `const SUPER_CALL_RULE: &str = "super_method";` (doc: the EVIDENCE rule graph's resolve_inherited_calls stamps on a `super.m()` bind; engine/tests/dead_dispatch.rs pins both ends) and `fn is_super_call(e: &Edge) -> bool { e.category == edge_category::CALLS && Evidence::of(e).and_then(|ev| ev.rule).as_deref() == Some(SUPER_CALL_RULE) }` (use glia_code_domain::evidence::Evidence). Evidence is read only for edges into a METHOD that is the `to` of a METHOD -> METHOD IMPLEMENTS, lazily. `callers` (used by the other categories) is unchanged; maps are looked up, never iterated into output. Module doc table, dead_symbol row: ".. not a METHOD that IMPLEMENTS a METHOD with an incoming CALLS / USES from a third node, neither of the two (dispatch reaches it, CH.1c; a `super.m()` call, rule super_method, dispatches to no override, DF.22c), ..".
5. engine/src/coverage.rs COVERAGE_CAVEATS, a typescript CALLS row (after the typescript HTTP_CALLS row): note "a method call on a value (`x.m()`, `this.x.m()`) binds through the declared class of a field (a typed field, a constructor parameter, `inject(T)`), a parameter, or a local annotated with a class, initialised by `new T(..)` / `inject(T)` / `x as T` or aliasing `this.<field>`: to that class's own method, else to the nearest superclass method; an override is reached from the member a call binds through method-level IMPLEMENTS (dispatch), never as a call edge. It stays unresolved when the receiver is typed by an interface (TypeScript interface members are not nodes), by a call's result (`const s = make()`), by a chain (`a.b.m()`), when it is a module-level const read inside a function, or when one body binds the name to two types (callbacks share their enclosing function's scope)"; verify "grep the method name". Test typescript_calls_caveat_names_the_receiver_gaps: row_note("src/app.ts", "typescript", "CALLS") contains "parameter", "nearest superclass", "IMPLEMENTS", "interface" and "a call's result"; a repo holding only main.go has no typescript CALLS row.
