Kina backend/internal/signer/signer.go has three layers:
- `func (s *PlatformSigner) submitTo(ctx context.Context, chainName, contractAddr, method string, args ...any)` parses escrowManagerABI (a raw-string JSON const of 8 functions, all EscrowManager's with matching arities: verified) and calls `parsedABI.Pack(method, args...)` (signer.go:201);
- `submit(ctx, chainName, method string, args ...any)` forwards with `s.submitTo(ctx, chainName, "", method, args...)`;
- eight public methods call `s.submit(ctx, chainName, "resolveDispute" | "proposeResolution" | "acceptResolution" | "enterPenaltyHold" | "claimTimeoutForBuyer" | "freeze" | "unfreeze" | "seizeToSink", ..)`.
After DT.10e the Pack site is non-literal (counted dynamic), so the platform signer's whole on-chain surface stays invisible. The identity lives at the wrapper's call sites, the CA.4 situation (engine/src/external/infer_wrappers.rs, Go collection wrappers). This reading stays inside the evm extractor's file scan rather than the overlay wrapper stage, because the wrapper's ABI comes from its body's receiver binding, which only this scan knows. Wrapper and callers sit in one file in the measured shape.

